# XSH pthread_kill()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Blocked-signal behaviour and the mask are tested with
# xsh/pthread_sigmask; the signal action as a whole is tested with
# xsh/2.4.3-signal-actions.

assertion  XSH.pthread_kill.001
anchor     functions/pthread_kill.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_kill.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       pthread_kill() of SIGUSR1 at another thread returns 0 and the
           handler (installed without SA_RESTART) runs in the target
           thread, not in the caller.
test       t/001-delivered-to-thread.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_kill.002
anchor     functions/pthread_kill.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2024
says       pthread_kill() with signal number 0 returns 0 or EINVAL and
           sends no signal (no handler runs).
note       Issue 8 makes EINVAL a may-fail error for sig 0; before, 0
           meant error checking only.
test       t/002-signal-zero.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_kill.003
anchor     functions/pthread_kill.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2008
says       pthread_kill() with a non-zero signal number that no system
           defines (-1 and INT_MAX) returns EINVAL.
test       t/003-invalid-signal.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_kill.004
anchor     functions/pthread_kill.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       pthread_kill() with the thread ID of a thread that has
           terminated but not been joined (a zombie thread) is not an
           error: with SIGUSR1 ignored it returns 0.
test       t/004-zombie-thread.c
